---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Receive">
  <h1>Receive offline</h1>
  <p>
    The Go recipient tool is in development; no signed downloadable receiver is
    available yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    The development receiver reads raw ciphertext and a local encrypted private
    PEM, prompts without echo, authenticates the message and saves exact
    plaintext to a new private Windows file. It never asks you to upload private
    material to this website. Use synthetic data until the release gates close.
  </p>
  <pre><code>.\zodiac-decrypt.exe decrypt --key ".\rsa-private-encrypted.pem" --in ".\ciphertext.txt" --out ".\message.txt"</code></pre>
  <p>
    Choose a new output name in a private, nonsynced folder on a local fixed
    disk. Existing files, network/device paths, drive aliases, streams and
    reparse redirection are rejected. The receiver preserves exact authenticated
    UTF-8 bytes and never prints plaintext or opens an editor. Saved files
    persist.
  </p>
  <p>
    Exit codes: 2 for usage/ciphertext format, 3 for key unlock, 4 for message
    authentication, 5 for local filesystem errors and 130 for cancellation. A
    failed authentication creates no output. Whitespace is not stripped from the
    raw file. Other receiver platforms are currently unsupported.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before any future release use, verify its publisher, signature and full file
    hash through an independently trusted channel. These checks, release custody
    guidance and integrated independent review are still pending.
  </p>
  <h2>Before the first message</h2>
  <p>
    The supported development target is Windows x64, version 0.0.1-dev. ARM64,
    other operating systems and a signed public download remain pending. Do not
    treat this development binary as a verified release.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a future release, obtain its full SHA-256 and expected signing publisher
    through a trusted independent channel. Check Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256
    and Get-AuthenticodeSignature on the executable before running it: require
    the expected full hash, Valid signature and matching publisher. Stop on
    missing or mismatched evidence; do not bypass Windows warnings. The release
    publisher/channel has not yet been established.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep the executable separately from the private key. Use the{' '}
    <a href="/keys/">offline key setup guide</a> once. The final key file has
    the ENCRYPTED PRIVATE KEY header, and the shareable public file has PUBLIC
    KEY. Check the actual pair locally:
  </p>
  <pre><code>&amp; 'C:\Verified Tools\zodiac-decrypt.exe' verify-key --key 'D:\Private Zodiac Keys\rsa-private-encrypted.pem' --public 'D:\Private Zodiac Keys\rsa-public.pem'</code></pre>
  <p>
    Enter the final passphrase at the hidden local console prompt. Expect
    RSA-3072 or RSA-4096 and the full SHA-256 SPKI fingerprint. Compare it with
    the sender’s Recipient details and the independently verified recipient
    identity. A wrong pair fails; a filename does not prove identity. No private
    contents or password need to be pasted into the site.
  </p>
  <h2>Readiness and encrypted backup drill</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Exchange your verified public PEM and full fingerprint with the sender
      through the existing trusted channel. Ask for a harmless test message
      whose exact text you can confirm.
    </li>
    <li>
      The sender downloads raw ciphertext.txt. Transfer that exact file: no
      added newline, whitespace, BOM, wrapping or artwork substitution.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run the decrypt command above with quoted local paths and a new output
      filename. Enter the final passphrase at the hidden prompt. Success reports
      only that a private plaintext file was saved. It does not print the
      message or open an editor.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open the saved file yourself and confirm the exact nonsecret test text
      through the trusted channel. The output persists on disk; decide who may
      access it and how long to retain it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Restore the final encrypted key backup into a separate restricted
      nonsynced local folder. Run verify-key against the original public PEM,
      compare the full fingerprint, and decrypt the same test file to another
      new output. Test the separately backed-up passphrase too. Keep the
      original key until this drill succeeds.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Readiness proves the tested recipient could decrypt that message; it does
    not authenticate every future sender. The receiver requires one command per
    message, with no OpenSSL steps per message. CLI friction and independent
    nontechnical usability trials remain open.
  </p>
  <h2>If a step fails</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Exit 2: usage, public pair or raw format</dt>
    <dd>
      Check quoted flags and filenames, select the matching public PEM, and
      obtain a fresh exact raw download. The tool deliberately does not strip
      whitespace.
    </dd>
    <dt>Exit 3: unable to unlock</dt>
    <dd>
      Check the final encrypted key and its exact ASCII passphrase. A wrong
      password, damaged key or unsupported encryption profile shares the same
      local error. Do not upload it to support.
    </dd>
    <dt>Exit 4: message cannot authenticate</dt>
    <dd>
      Check the intended recipient key and request a fresh raw file. Wrong keys
      and tampering cannot be distinguished reliably. No plaintext output is
      created.
    </dd>
    <dt>Exit 5: local file error</dt>
    <dd>
      Use an ordinary local fixed-disk folder, check access and choose a new
      output name. Existing files are never replaced. Avoid cloud redirection,
      junctions, network shares, devices and alternate streams.
    </dd>
    <dt>Exit 130: canceled</dt>
    <dd>
      Esc or Ctrl+C cancels. Retry when ready using a new output name if a prior
      operation completed.
    </dd>
  </dl>
</Layout>
